      "content": "Okay, I hear you on the legal/redlining aspect. I'm definitely trying to build something compliant. But from a pure data science perspective, if we are training a model on historical datasets, those datasets are going to contain these correlations. If the model is meant to accurately mirror real-world outcomes, it's going to \"notice\" that certain areas have different default frequencies.\n\nLet's strip the \"discrimination\" label off for a second and just look at it as a regression problem. If a model identifies a high correlation between a specific zip code and a high rate of delinquency, is it technically \"biased\" to include that variable in a predictive model, or is it just being mathematically accurate to the training data? I'm trying to understand the tension between statistical accuracy and algorithmic fairness in these kinds of machine learning models.",
